{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>When you go out to war against your enemy.</b> The verse speaks of an obligatory war. And Scripture juxtaposed the going out to war to the verse above it, ״נפש בנפש עין בעין״ — a life for a life, an eye for an eye — to teach you that one who is missing a limb does not go out to war. And furthermore, to tell you that if you have practiced justice and righteousness, you are assured that if you go out to war you will be victorious; thus David says (Psalms 119:121): ״עשיתי משפט וצדק בל תניחני לעושקי״." "<b>Do not fear them, for HaShem your Elohim is with you.</b> And it is fitting that the fear of HaShem — may He be exalted — prevail over the fear of man. And He further added ״אל תיראו ואל תחפזו״ — do not fear and do not panic — for all who fear flesh and blood are as though they forget HaShem — may He be exalted — as the prophet said (Isaiah 51:12): ״מי את ותיראי מאנוש ימות״ — who are you that you should fear man who dies, etc. — ״ותשכח ה' עושך״ — and you forget HaShem your Maker; and Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 29:25): ״חרדת אדם יתן מוקש ובוטח בה' ישוגב״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {1 "The abbreviation הש\"י expanded as 'HaShem — may He be exalted' (השם יתברך), following standard rabbinic convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:34.658358593Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}